{
  // The type tests are only tests if something typechecks them. The package's own `tsconfig.json`
  // builds `src` alone and `vitest run` executes without checking types, so every `expectTypeOf` in
  // `tests/` was decorative. That is how a relation cycle that could not compile went unnoticed.
  //
  // Every file in `tests/` is checked now. Getting the last two in (2026-08-14) meant fixing what they
  // were actually pinning, which was not an outdated `expectTypeOf` API:
  //
  //   - Reading *any* `Optional` field was `TS2589`. A brand is an intersection carrying an optional
  //     phantom property, so every type structurally satisfied `Modifier<Name, infer _>`: a plain
  //     `string` reported as `Optional`, and unwrapping never reached a fixed point.
  //   - `Optional<FileAsset<B>>` resolved to `never`, because each unwrap step distributed over the
  //     union and dropped the branches that were not object types.
  //   - Stacked modifiers lost their tag to an intersection, so only structural matching could see the
  //     outer one, which was the false positive again.
  //
  // `Modifier` is now a nominal wrapper with a required discriminator, the pattern the relation types
  // already used. Two tests in `primitives.test.ts` also contradicted each other: one asserted that
  // `Owner<"author_id">` works on a plain `author_id` column, another that it is rejected as not
  // relation-backed. The second is the current contract.
  //
  // This file sits in `tests/` rather than beside the package's `tsconfig.json` so that it is the
  // nearest tsconfig to every test, which is how the lint's project service finds a file's types.
  "extends": "../../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["../src", "."]
}
